# Every consumer of this repository is Linux: the three service VMs, the
# systemd units and shell scripts rendered onto them, the Ansible that renders
# them, and the GitHub Actions runner. No .bat, .cmd or .ps1 file is tracked
# here, so nothing in the tree wants CRLF.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config
# (C:/Program Files/Git/etc/gitconfig). Every blob in this repo's object
# database is LF, but that setting gave the Windows workstation a CRLF working
# tree on checkout, and `make validate` could not pass there at all: yamllint's
# new-lines rule rejects every YAML file, so validate-yaml failed identically
# whatever the change was. A gate that fails the same way for every input is
# not measuring anything -- the same "check nobody can tell is broken" failure
# this repo worries about in scan.yml and the credential canary.
#
# eol=lf pins the working tree to LF on every platform and takes precedence
# over core.autocrlf, so the gate measures the change instead of the checkout.
# It travels with the repo, which a local `git config` would not.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The only tracked binary. text=auto would very probably detect it on its own,
# but "very probably" is not what should stand between a line-ending rewrite
# and the one file in this repo it could silently corrupt.
